 the town's prestige  since the protests started a number of  statues and busts have been taken down  but authorities are busy restoring  others this history professor thinks  that sends the wrong message  the main problem of belgium though and  this is again a difference with  other former colonial metropoles is that  we do not have  any monuments for victims of the  colonization or  heroes of anti-colonial resistance  anvetzi is collecting her son she wants  him to learn  as much as possible about his family's  history  her own mother still remembers the  brutal reality of colonial rule  in the mid 20th century back in africa  our parents didn't want us to see them  being treated badly  they would tell us not to come close  but i was curious i would hide and ask  myself  why people were being hit  and one day i asked my dad if he'd been  hit  he said yes and told me that he'd been  punished  because he didn't have enough money to  pay the taxes  on betsy knows that many parents of  congolese descent do not like to share  unpleasant memories but for her son  and the generations to come she feels  transparency  is the best way forward  [Music]  all those family secrets are passed down  from generation to generation  and it weighs on people but when you say  things clearly  this allows future generations to cope  with the past  so they don't become victims of what  remains in our collective consciousness  the situation is  the exact opposite is what you find in  colonial art  a glorification of this time in the  africa museum in brussels  most of the colonial art has been  confined to a small room  in the basement but as long as people  from that era are still alive wedzie  feels  that's not enough to them is just  oh look at our past how glorious it was  maybe we should put all the statues away  from them for the moment  maybe wait that those people disappear  and then show them again but with  another message  and just with the real message that  the belgium didn't